Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Chamfers: a chamfer over a narrow face whose chain runs on across a slight
concave crease of its other face, and the faces beyond a narrow face grown
past the bounds of their B-spline surfaces (mitcad#343).

The case: a fin 1 mm thick whose top edges, an arc and a profile's spline
stretch in turn, meet at vertices where the top faces kink by 1.4 and 2.2
degrees (concave creases); both top chains are chamfered by 0.263 at 45
degrees, and the strips of the fin's sides between the top edges and the
root's chamfer (a walked B-spline: a plane and a torus) are narrower than
the chamfer along the spline stretches (patch 0531's case). The chamfer
failed with an exception before any part of it was made.

- Patch 0980's crease turn (StartSol: a face taken for tangent that meets
  the face before at a concave crease of more than the angular tolerance
  is no continuation; the ball turns about the crease's edge, rolling on
  it) took the chamfer's piece after the arc's analytic one for a blend
  rolling on the crease's edge, which chamfers do not have ("PerformSurf
  Not Implemented"); no piece of the chamfer was left for
  ChamferOverNarrowFace to find the narrow face by. A chamfer's contact,
  at its distance along the face, passes such a crease onto the next face:
  the crease turn is now a rounding's only.
- With the walk through, ChamferOverNarrowFace computes the chamfer again
  over the narrow face's surface unbounded; patch 0671's
  ChFi3d_KPartCutChainToEnd then asked whether the arc's analytic piece
  takes material off by the spine's edge with its faces, one of them the
  unbounded face that does not hold the edge, and OCCT threw
  (BRepTools::OriEdgeInFace). It now looks only at faces of the spine's
  edge (ChFi3d_EdgeOfFaces); the narrow-face computation cuts the chamfer
  by the faces beyond itself.
- The root's chamfer, a B-spline, grows up to the tip chamfer along its
  sections continued straight past its bounds (0531's ChFi3d_WideSurface),
  but the face kept its surface, so the face ran past its surface's
  parameter bounds (by 17 of 82 there): its geometry right where OCCT
  evaluates the B-spline past its bounds, but tools that keep to the
  bounds (ShapeAnalysis_Surface's projections) found its points nowhere
  on it. ChFi3d_Builder::Compute now gives each face of the result that
  runs past its non-rational B-spline surface's bounds in a direction of
  degree 1 that surface continued linearly up to the face's bounds
  (ChFi3d_ContinueGrownFaces): its end row of poles moved along the end
  span's lines, the knot moved with it, the parameters as they were, and
  each edge's curve on the face stored again with the continued surface
  (an edge's curve on a face is kept with the face's surface).

On the design's body before the item, replayed and stored: valid, the
file's faces (124 on the stored body; the replayed one has a face more
elsewhere) every one within 3e-4 mm of the result's and the result's on
the file's within 1e-3 mm; before, the chamfer failed.

Upstream: new.
